WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:04.445
PostgreSQL offers USING as a convenient alternative
to ON when both tables

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:10.258
share the same join-key name In this query both
patients and admissions

00:00:10.271 --> 00:00:15.800
contain patient ID Writing USING patient ID tells
PostgreSQL to match

00:00:15.826 --> 00:00:18.984
the columns with that name and return a single
join-key column in

00:00:19.011 --> 00:00:24.359
the result Run the query and compare its result with
the earlier

00:00:24.385 --> 00:00:29.889
INNER JOIN The relationship is the same USING

00:00:29.916 --> 00:00:32.981
can make a simple join shorter and easier to read
but ON

00:00:32.995 --> 00:00:37.037
is more flexible Use ON when the key names differ
when you

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.629
need more than one matching condition or when the
relationship uses an

00:00:40.655 --> 00:00:46.382
expression The goal is clarity Do not force USING

00:00:46.408 --> 00:00:51.290
into a query when it makes the relationship less
obvious Whichever syntax

00:00:51.316 --> 00:00:54.395
you choose you should still be able to explain the
matching rule

00:00:54.408 --> 00:00:55.290
in one sentence
